# locale/en/messages -- the SOURCE LANGUAGE of Osum's surface.
#
# Installed to /usr/share/locale/en/messages. Plain text, readable with
# `cat`, one entry per line:
#
#     key = text
#
# THE KEY IS ALWAYS ENGLISH AND IS NEVER TRANSLATED. It is a name, like
# a path or a command, and the rule of this system is that names stay
# English -- see docs/I18N.md, first section. What gets translated is
# the text on the right, and nothing else.
#
# ENGLISH IS ALSO THE FALLBACK. A key that a translation does not have
# keeps the text from this file. That is why this file defines the key
# set: kernel/user/msg.fi reads it FIRST and every other language is
# laid over it. A key that exists only in a translation is ignored and
# counted, because it cannot mean anything -- there is no English text
# it could stand in for.
#
# ESCAPES: \n is a line break, \t a tab, \\ a backslash. A menu is one
# entry per line, a table header one column per tab -- that is what the
# widget library expects, and it is why the escapes exist at all.
#
# PATHS INSIDE A TEXT STAY AS THEY ARE. "/etc/schemas" below is not
# translated in any language. Windows shows "Program Files" as
# "Programme" in German and then no command finds it; this system does
# not repeat that.

